children.translated_text:
{Her limbs languid from crushed lotus-stalks, her activity somehow
continues through the entreaties of her attendants in [daily] tasks;
and the moonbeam-like beauty of the spotless moon is matched
by her cheek, lovely as a freshly-cut elephant tusk. //28//}

{When the beloved arrives eagerly from afar — turned [toward him]; when he speaks — wide open;
when he embraces — reddened; when the garment is grasped — with slightly arched brows;
when the offending lover falls at the proud woman's feet — filled with tears;
the eye has become, alas, wonderfully skilled in display! //29//}

{Seeing the bedchamber empty, rising quietly from the bed,
long gazing at the face of her husband who came feigning sleep;
then confidently kissing him, seeing the gooseflesh on his cheek,
the young woman, face bent down in modesty, was long kissed by the laughing beloved. //30//}

{At the first offense of her husband, without the instruction of a friend,
she knows not the coquettish turning of limbs, the insinuation of reproachful speech;
with clear tears flowing from the base of her cheeks, her lotus-eyes overturned,
the young woman simply weeps, with her disheveled curls and streaming tears. //34//}

{Her bangles announced the departure; tears went forth ceaselessly, dear friends;
courage did not sit even for a moment; the mind resolved to go ahead;
when the beloved set his determined mind to go, all set forth together —
since the journey must be made, O life, why should the caravan of the dear friend be abandoned? //35//}

{Having drawn you on a rock with mineral colors, angered by love,
while I wish to portray myself fallen at your feet —
my sight is obscured by tears gathering again and again;
cruelly, even in that [painting], fate does not permit our union! //36//}

{Contracting her hand, the courtesan placed it on my head, impure with pseudo-mantras,
sprinkling drops at every step on [my] holy person;
she loudly delivered a blow with a prominent clapping sound —
'Alas, alas, I am struck!' cries <s>Viṣṇuśarman</s>! //37//}

{With his gaze fixed on the chariot that repeatedly follows [the deer], its neck-bending charming,
the [deer's] front body thrust forward and rear body entered [the thicket], out of fear of the arrow's fall;
with half-chewed blades of grass falling from its mouth gaping with exertion, scattering the path —
see! Because of leaping upward, it travels much in the sky and little on the earth. //41//}

{Your vision, which now removes sin and is the cause of jealousy,
which through former deeds has produced merit through meritorious acts —
the vision of you, for embodied beings, reveals worthiness across the three times. //46//}

children.text:
vāctavtañjyayoḥ niḥśeṣetyādau niṣedhavidhyātmanā
  {mātsaryamutsārya vicārya kāryamāryāḥ samaryādamudāharantu /
  sevyānitambāḥ kimubhūdharāṇāmutasmarasmeravilāsinīnām //133//}

children.translated_text:
Regarding the expressed and suggested [meanings], in [examples like] 'complete cessation' and the like, in the form of prohibition and injunction —
{'Setting aside envy, having deliberated the matter,
let the noble ones pronounce with propriety:
should the slopes to be frequented be those of mountains
or those of women lovely with the smile of love? //133//'}

{'The gaze of the mountain-king, even though he had a son, did not attain satisfaction
regarding that offspring; for the garland of double-bees has a special attachment
to the mango of the bee [Madhu, i.e., Spring], which has inexhaustible flowers. //247//'}

{'Some, covered with dust, produced in the sky a moon-beauty of faint luster;
some, their luster gone, held burning within, like interiors of directions, with creatures in panic;
others whirled like whirlwinds; others, trembling like the earth, at every step —
at the departure of the kings, the women declared the inauspicious future. //249//'}

{'Let the buffaloes plunge into the watering-pool water, struck repeatedly by their horns;
let the herd of deer, clustered in the shade, practice rumination;
let the boar-chiefs confidently make scratch-marks of <s>mustā</s> grass in the marsh;
and let this bow of ours, with loosened bowstring, find rest. //250//'}

{'Power on your pair of arms — conquerable by the sword; O lord, the beauty of the moon-maker [<s>Śiva</s>]
on [your] face; and at [your] side dwells this great procuress — the sword-staff;
your command pervades everywhere; and before [you] — what use of me, an old woman, to you?
Saying thus in anger, by his fame, whitened as the moon's rays, [she] departed!' //253//'}

{'Struck in the heart by the arrow of <s>Rāma</s>'s love (<s>Rāma-Manmatha</s>), unbearably,
the <s>Rākṣasī</s> (demoness), smeared with fragrant blood-sandal,
went to the abode of the Lord of Life [i.e., died]. //254//'}

{'In the world, your qualities triumph — the crescent-moon digit and the rest,
naturally sweet; there are indeed others that intoxicate the mind;
but for me, since this moonlight of the eyes has come into the world,
that alone is the single great festival for the eyes in this birth! //257//'}

